aebf5b9f (1523 1793425979819 "guenchi" (("4gm4c2jr" . 3240) ("w5vbequy" . 14982)) (put ((kind . section) (title . "landing f366") (src . "languages: .tsx and .jsx entries whose scan follows JSX\n\nA .tsx or .jsx file had no language entry: its symbols went unchecked\nand the file was imported whole, with no cut. Two entries are added,\ntypescriptreact (tsx) and javascriptreact (jsx), named as the editor\nnames them, with no runner, the function head also taking export\ndefault and async, and a profile that asks the ecmascript scan to\nfollow JSX.\n\nIn those profiles a \"<\" in code where an operand is expected, followed\npast trivia by a name or \">\", opens an element, where plain JavaScript\nrefuses the file: the opening tag is a token set (names, \".\" and \":\",\n\"=\", attribute strings with no escape character that may span lines,\n\"{\" containers, \"/\" and \">\"), and a closing tag or a self-closing end\na smaller one (\"/\", names, \".\" and \":\", \">\"), with trivia between any\ntwo tokens, where trivia is whitespace, line ends and both comment\nforms and a block comment returns to the state it began in; children\nrun to \"<\" or \"{\", and \">\" or \"}\" as text refuses the file. An element\nis a frame on the stack, so no line inside one is a cut; when it ends\nthe scan is back in the parent's children when an element is still\nopen, else in code with an expression ended. The arrangement of a\ntag's tokens is not checked and closers match by depth. In .tsx a \"<\"\nfirst goes through TypeScript's own test for a JSX file: an optional\nconst and one identifier, then \",\" or a single \"=\" (not \"==\" or \"=>\"),\nor extends not followed by \"=\", \">\" or \"/\" (each a single token),\nmeans type parameters, which the scan refuses at the \"<\". Type\narguments on a tag name, an element as an attribute's value, a \"<\"\nfollowed by neither a name nor \">\" (a closing tag with no element\nopen among them), a character no tag token starts with, and a\nfunction type where an operand is expected are refused too, each at\nthe first offset any live reading meets it. The plain-JavaScript rules\nand the self-close rule are unchanged, the self-close rule applying in\nthe jsx profiles as well. The limits and the cost are named in the\nscan's header; the README says what the three families of profiles do\nwith JSX, lists the jsx refusals under symbols-unscanned, and names\nthe two entries among the languages with no runner.\n\nTests: ecmascript-scan gains the jsx rows (a component file cut at its\ndefinition lines only; the element-ended return; names, strings,\ncomments and trivia at every tag token boundary; containers; type\npositions; every refusal at its offset; the speculative-reading cost;\nthe limit where an operator is expected; the coverage row; a symbols\nimport, whose helper now names the source's language id by extension;\nthe entries and the no-runner answer); text-heads and name-use-verbs\ntake the two entries; import-symbols' row for a file with no language\nentry moves to an extension that still has none, and a new row has a\n.tsx file's symbols checked and the file cut at its definition lines;\nrows-baseline.txt carries ecmascript-scan's and import-symbols' new\ncounts and hashes and name-use-verbs' new hash.\n") (parent . root) (ord . 564))))
