tion type where an operand is expected are refused too, each at\nthe first offset any live reading meets it. The plain-JavaScript rules\nand the self-close rule are unchanged, the self-close rule applying in\nthe jsx profiles as well. The limits and the cost are named in the\nscan's header; the README says what the three families of profiles do\nwith JSX, lists the jsx refusals under symbols-unscanned, and names\nthe two entries among the languages with no runner.\n\nTests: ecmascript-scan gains the jsx rows (a component file cut at its\ndefinition lines only; the element-ended return; names, strings,\ncomments and trivia at every tag token boundary; containers; type\npositions; every refusal at its offset; the speculative-reading cost;\nthe limit where an operator is expected; the coverage row; a symbols\nimport, whose helper now names the source's language id by extension;\nthe entries and the no-runner answer); text-heads and name-use-verbs\ntake the two entries; import-symbols' row for a file with no language\nentry moves to an extension that still has none, and a new row has a\n.tsx file's symbols checked and the file cut at its definition lines;\nrows-baseline.txt carries ecmascript-scan's and import-symbols' new\ncounts and hashes and name-use-verbs' new hash.\n") (parent . root) (ord . 565))))
